A command has exactly one handler. It is an ordinary class — no base class, no registration —
and the generator finds it through the contract it implements.

## With a result

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

## With no result

```csharp
public sealed class ArchiveOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<ArchiveOrder>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(ArchiveOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => await repository.ArchiveAsync(command.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Dependencies

Constructor parameters are resolved from the **dispatching scope**, so a scoped
`IOrderRepository` behaves exactly as it does in a controller. Nothing special is needed:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(
    IOrderRepository repository,
    IPricingService pricing,
    ILogger<PlaceOrderHandler> logger) : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid> { … }
```

{% aside type="tip" %}
A handler with a single public constructor and plain service parameters can be constructed
directly by a compiled plan, skipping the container entirely. Several public constructors keep
it on the container path and are reported as `ERGO003` — informational, since everything still
works.
{% /aside %}

## Always pass the cancellation token

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
```

This is the most common omission, and it only shows up under load.

## Dispatching

```csharp
public sealed class OrdersController(ICommandMediator mediator)
{
    public async Task<IResult> Post(PlaceOrder command, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command, ct);
        return Results.Created($"/orders/{id}", id);
    }
}
```

The overloads:

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command);                              // no result
await mediator.SendAsync(command, ct);
var id = await mediator.SendAsync(new PlaceOrder(…));           // with result
var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command, GroupSet.Of("audit"), ct);
await mediator.SendAsync(command, context);                     // share a context
```

When both the message and result types are known at the call site, naming them lets the
dispatch build its executor from arguments the compiler already resolved rather than
constructing it reflectively:

```csharp
var id = await mediator.SendAsync<PlaceOrder, Guid>(command, ct);
```

That matters for NativeAOT rather than for throughput — the two paths measure within a
nanosecond of each other.

## Covariant handlers

A handler registered against a base type serves every command in that family:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderCommandHandler : ICommandHandler<OrderCommand> { … }
```

A **direct** handler always wins over a covariant one, with no ambiguity — that is the idiom for
a default with specific overrides:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder> { … }   // wins for PlaceOrder
```

Two handlers at the same level is `ERGO010`, a build error, because the ladder has no
tiebreaker within a level.

## Handlers are not where cross-cutting work goes

Logging, validation, authorization and metrics belong in interceptors — otherwise you write
them again in the next handler. See
[Interceptor or handler](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/interceptor-or-handler).

## Testing one

Construct it, because it is an ordinary class:

```csharp
var handler = new PlaceOrderHandler(new InMemoryOrderRepository());
var id = await handler.HandleAsync(new PlaceOrder(customerId, lines), new ErgosfareContext());
```

That tests the logic. To test that the handler is *reachable* — that its interceptors run and
the dispatch resolves — build a container and dispatch. See
[Composition root and testing](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/composition-root).

An event handler — a subscriber — reacts to something that already happened. Every matching one
runs, and none of them answers the publisher.

## Writing one

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class SendShipmentEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<OrderShipped>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderShipped @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync(@event.CustomerEmail, "Your order shipped", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

`IEventHandler<TEvent>` returns `ValueTask` and nothing else — a broadcast has no result to
produce.

## Many subscribers, one event

```csharp
public sealed class SendShipmentEmail  : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
public sealed class UpdateReadModel    : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
public sealed class AuditOrderEvents   : IEventHandler<OrderEvent>   { … }
```

Publishing an `OrderShipped` runs all three. Unlike commands, this is not a contest — `ERGO010`
does not apply to events, because delivering to everyone is the point.

They run in `[Weight]` order, direct registrations before covariant ones. Ordering exists, but
relying on it between subscribers is a smell: if B must run after A, they are probably one
operation.

## When one throws

The exception propagates out of the publish. Subscribers that had already run stay run;
subscribers after it do not.

That is rarely what you want, so a subscriber that can fail should contain its own failure:

```csharp
public sealed class SendShipmentEmail(IEmailSender sender, ILogger<SendShipmentEmail> logger)
    : IEventHandler<OrderShipped>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderShipped @event, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        try
        {
            await sender.SendAsync(@event.CustomerEmail, "Your order shipped", context.CancellationToken);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            logger.LogError(ex, "shipment email failed for {Order}", @event.OrderId);
        }
    }
}
```

An exception interceptor on the event pipeline can do this centrally instead. Either way, decide
deliberately — the default is that one failing subscriber stops the rest.

## Publishing

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new OrderShipped(id, email, at));
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, ct);
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, GroupSet.Of("audit"), ct);
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, context);          // share the dispatching context
```

A publish returns when every subscriber has completed. It is not fire-and-forget: if you want
that, put the work on a queue and let a consumer dispatch it.

{% aside type="note" %}
Every publish requires at least one selected main handler. When the compiler can prove
otherwise, `ERGO005` or `ERGO024` fails the build. Dynamic group sets selecting no handler
throw `NoHandlerFoundException` before interceptors run.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing from a handler

Common, and the shape worth getting right:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository, IEventMediator events)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);

        await events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(order.Id), context);
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

Passing `context` keeps the publish in the same logical operation — same items, same
cancellation.

**Watch the transaction boundary.** Publishing before a commit lets a subscriber act on state
that later rolls back. Collect the events and publish after committing — see the
[Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## Subscribers get the full pipeline

Interceptors apply to events like anything else: `IEventPreInterceptor`,
`IEventPostInterceptor`, `IEventExceptionInterceptor`, `IEventFinalInterceptor`.

The pre and post seams name the boundary around a *delivery*, so in a broadcast they run once
per subscriber rather than once per publish.

## Registering

Nothing to write — `AddGenerated()` finds subscribers like any other participant:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

## Testing

```csharp
var sender = new RecordingEmailSender();
var handler = new SendShipmentEmail(sender);

await handler.HandleAsync(new OrderShipped(id, "a@b.c", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow),
                          new ErgosfareContext());

Assert.Single(sender.Sent);
```

To test that *all* the subscribers you expect run, build a container and publish — that is the
part a unit test cannot tell you.

A query has exactly one handler, found through the contract it implements.

## A single result

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

## A stream

The method is `StreamAsync`, and it takes the query and the context:

```csharp
public sealed class SearchProductsHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IStreamQueryHandler<SearchProducts, ProductDto>
{
    public async IAsyncEnumerable<ProductDto> StreamAsync(
        SearchProducts query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        await foreach (var product in repository.SearchAsync(query.Term, context.CancellationToken))
            yield return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

Cancellation comes from `context.CancellationToken`, so there is no separate token parameter to
forget — pass it into whatever you enumerate.

{% aside type="note" %}
A `null` element is a legitimate element. If your sequence is `IAsyncEnumerable<ProductDto?>`
and you yield `null`, the caller receives it — the pipeline does not filter nulls out.
{% /aside %}

## Dispatching

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id), ct);
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id), GroupSet.Of("cached"), ct);
```

And a stream:

```csharp
await foreach (var product in mediator.StreamAsync(new SearchProducts("bench"))
                                      .WithCancellation(ct))
{
    …
}
```

When both types are known at the call site, naming them keeps the dispatch from constructing its
executor reflectively — which matters for NativeAOT rather than throughput:

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync<GetProduct, ProductDto>(new GetProduct(id), ct);
```

The streaming members stay untyped for now: their shape is under revision, and adding a surface
to something scheduled to change is work that has to be undone.

## Keep queries read-only

Nothing enforces it, and that is the point of having two modules — a query that writes is a
command that lied about itself. It also breaks reasonable assumptions: a caching interceptor on
the query pipeline is safe precisely because queries do not have effects.

## Dependencies

Constructor parameters come from the dispatching scope, like any other service. A read-only
`DbContext`, a Dapper connection factory, a cache — all ordinary registrations.

## Covariant handlers

A handler on a base query type serves the family, and a direct handler wins outright:

```csharp
public sealed class TenantQueryHandler<TResult> : IQueryHandler<TenantQuery<TResult>, TResult> { … }
```

Two handlers at the same level is `ERGO010`.

## Testing

```csharp
var handler = new GetProductHandler(new InMemoryProductRepository(seed));
var dto = await handler.HandleAsync(new GetProduct(id), new ErgosfareContext());
```

For a stream, drain it:

```csharp
var results = new List<ProductDto>();
await foreach (var item in handler.StreamAsync(query, new ErgosfareContext()))
    results.Add(item);
```

Every handler and interceptor receives an `ErgosfareContext`. It carries the cancellation token,
a bag of per-dispatch state, and the way to stop a pipeline.

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    …
}
```

## Cancellation

`context.CancellationToken` is the token the dispatch was started with. Pass it down rather
than reaching for `CancellationToken.None`:

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
```

Every mediator call takes a token, and the ones that do not take one explicitly default it:

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command, cancellationToken);
```

## Per-dispatch state

`Items` is a dictionary shared by every participant in one dispatch. It is how a pre-interceptor
tells a later stage something without changing the message:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("startedAt", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

The accessors:

| Member | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Set(key, value)` | Stores a value. |
| `Has(key)` | Whether a key is present. |
| `Get<T>(key)` | Reads it, throwing when absent or the wrong type. |
| `TryGet<T>(key, out value)` | Reads it without throwing. |
| `Items` | The underlying dictionary, for anything the above does not cover. |

{% aside type="caution" %}
`Items` is per dispatch, not per request. A nested dispatch gets its own context unless you
pass yours explicitly, so it is the wrong place for something an HTTP request scope should own.
Put that in a scoped service and inject it.
{% /aside %}

## Stopping the pipeline

`Abort()` ends the dispatch immediately:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (_tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("no tenant on this request");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

It throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, and the pipeline treats that as a stop rather than a
failure: **the exception stage does not run, and neither does the final stage.** The signal
travels to the caller unchanged.

That distinction is the whole point of having a separate signal. A failure is something that
went wrong and that a final interceptor should still observe; an abort is a decision that this
dispatch should not continue.

Overloads carry a reason and a value:

```csharp
context.Abort("not authorized");
context.Abort("quota exceeded", remainingQuota);
```

## Passing a context along

Every dispatch surface takes an optional context. Passing yours makes the nested dispatch share
your `Items` and your cancellation:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(command.Id), context);
}
```

Omitting it gives the nested dispatch a fresh context instead. Which you want depends on whether
the inner work is part of the same logical operation — see
[Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Scopes

`CreateScope()` gives a nested lifetime within one dispatch, for state that should not outlive
a section of the pipeline:

```csharp
using var scope = context.CreateScope();
```

## Why it is pooled

Contexts are pooled and returned when the dispatch completes. That is what keeps a dispatch from
allocating one per call on the hot path, and it has one consequence worth knowing:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Do not keep a context after your participant returns.** Storing it in a field, closing over it
in a background task, or handing it to something that outlives the dispatch means you are
holding an object that has already been returned to the pool and may now belong to an unrelated
dispatch. Copy the values you need instead.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// wrong — the context is gone by the time this runs
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(context.Get<string>("user")));

// right — read it first
var user = context.Get<string>("user");
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(user));
```

The pooling is also why there is no `AsyncLocal` anywhere in the dispatch path: the context is a
parameter, so it flows exactly as far as you pass it and no further.

Interceptors are how cross-cutting work attaches to a dispatch without the handler knowing
about it. They are discovered and registered exactly like handlers, and they run as part of the
same compiled pipeline.

## The four stages

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler, and may rewrite the message.
- **post** runs after a successful handler, and may rewrite the result.
- **exception** runs when the handler or an interceptor threw, and may supply a result instead.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage, order is `[Weight]` descending, with direct registrations before covariant ones.
See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptors

A pre-interceptor returns the message the next stage will see. Returning the input unchanged is
the pass-through case:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Two arities are available, trading breadth for type safety:

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, ErgosfareContext)` | it applies to every command |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, ErgosfareContext)` | it applies to one command or hierarchy |

The typed form returns the typed message, so rewriting needs no cast:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

Returning a *derived* message is allowed and often the point — `TCommand` is the contract, and
anything assignable to it satisfies that:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command));
}
```

{% aside type="note" %}
Earlier previews had a two-parameter form, `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>`. It
was removed: since the one-parameter form started returning the typed message, the second
argument bought nothing. Migration is a deletion — drop the second type argument and the
interceptor keeps returning what it returned.
{% /aside %}

## Post-interceptors

A post-interceptor sees a successful result and may replace it:

```csharp
public sealed class StampCreatedAt : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Three arities:

| Interface | Signature |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, ErgosfareContext)` |

For a void pipeline the result slot carries `Unit`, so a post-interceptor written against a
command with no result still has something well-defined to receive.

## Exception interceptors

An exception interceptor runs when something threw, and it can answer the dispatch with a result
instead of letting the exception escape:

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct command, ProductDto? result, Exception exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (exception is NotFoundException)
            return ValueTask.FromResult(ProductDto.Empty);

        throw exception;
    }
}
```

Note the asymmetry, which is deliberate: **the result comes in nullable and goes out
non-nullable.** The handler may have thrown before producing anything, so there genuinely may
be no result yet — but a dispatch locked its result type at the call site, and no stage
downstream may hand the caller a `null` it never asked for.

An interceptor that cannot produce a result should not claim the failure. Rethrow, or do not
match; an unmatched stage lets the exception surface. Code that wants to model absence models
it in the value, which is what a result carrier is for.

Once any exception interceptor has run, the exception is considered handled and the pipeline
continues to the final stage. If none matched, it surfaces.

### Filtering by exception type

Implementing the `For` variant narrows an interceptor to one exception type, which reads better
than an `is` check and lets the compiled plan bake the test:

```csharp
public sealed class HandleTimeouts
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptorFor<PlaceOrder, Guid, TimeoutException>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, TimeoutException exception, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(Guid.Empty);
}
```

## Final interceptors

A final interceptor always runs — after success, after a handled failure, after an unhandled
one. It returns nothing, because by then the dispatch's answer is settled:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both the result and the exception are nullable here, and for the same reason: it runs from a
`finally`, so either may be absent.

The one case where it does *not* run is a deliberate stop — see below.

## Stopping a pipeline

Any participant can end the dispatch early by throwing `ExecutionAbortedException`:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.IsAuthenticated)
            throw new ExecutionAbortedException("not authenticated");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

This is not a failure and is not treated as one: the exception stage does not run, the final
stage does not run, and the signal travels to the caller. It says "this dispatch should not
continue", not "this dispatch went wrong".

## Which module's contracts to use

Each module has its own interceptor contracts — `ICommandPreInterceptor`,
`IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — and they carry that module's marker, which is
how discovery finds them.

For something genuinely universal, implement the core contracts in
`Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Handlers` against `IMessage`. That reaches every module's
messages, at the cost of a weaker static type.

## What this costs

Nothing per dispatch that you did not ask for. The stages are assembled at compile time, and
for a pipeline whose shape is fully settled, the generated plan is a straight line of calls
with the interceptors in place — no descriptor list, no ordering work, and for participants
provably constructible, no container resolution either.

A pipeline with no interceptors at all pays for none: it is the same straight line without the
calls.

A final interceptor runs last, whether the dispatch succeeded or failed. It returns nothing,
because by the time it runs the dispatch's answer is already settled.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandFinalInterceptor                        // every command
ICommandFinalInterceptor<TCommand>              // one command
ICommandFinalInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>     // typed result
```

Queries have the same three; events have the ones without a result.

## The shape

```csharp
public sealed class RecordOutcome(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, succeeded: exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both the result and the exception are nullable, and both can be `null` at once — a void
pipeline that succeeded has neither a meaningful result nor a failure. Read them as a pair:

| `exception` | `result` | What happened |
|---|---|---|
| `null` | a value | the handler succeeded |
| `null` | `null` | a void pipeline succeeded |
| set | `null` | it failed and nothing answered |
| set | a value | it failed and the exception stage answered |

## The one case where it does not run

`context.Abort()`.

An abort is a deliberate stop rather than a failure, so it skips the exception stage **and this
one**, and travels to the caller unchanged.

{% aside type="caution" %}
This matters when a pre-interceptor opened something that the final stage was going to close.
If any interceptor can abort after that point, the resource leaks.

Two ways out: put the aborting check ahead of whatever opens the resource, with a higher
`[Weight]` — or open it in the handler where a `try`/`finally` covers every exit.
{% /aside %}

## Pairing with a pre-interceptor

The common shape is a pre-interceptor that records something and a final one that reads it:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class StopTimer(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("startedAt", out var started))
            metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Use `TryGet` rather than `Get`. The final interceptor may run for a dispatch where the pre one
never did — a different group, or an interceptor added later — and it should not throw while
cleaning up.

## Ordering

`[Weight]` descending, direct before covariant. When a final interceptor closes something,
give it a weight that puts it after anything that reads what it closes.

## Do not throw from here

An exception from a final interceptor escapes into whatever the pipeline was already doing. If
the dispatch was failing, you have replaced a meaningful failure with a bookkeeping one.

Wrap anything that can fail:

```csharp
try
{
    await _audit.WriteAsync(row, context.CancellationToken);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _logger.LogWarning(ex, "audit write failed");
}
```

Also be careful with the cancellation token here: if the dispatch failed *because* it was
cancelled, the token is already cancelled and any work you start with it fails immediately.

## Typical uses

**Timing and metrics** for every outcome.

**Audit rows** recording that an operation was attempted, with its result.

**Releasing something** a pre-interceptor took — subject to the abort caveat above.

**Committing or rolling back a transaction**, which is exactly the both-paths shape this stage
exists for. See the [Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## What does not belong here

**Changing the answer.** It returns `void`; the answer is settled. Use post or exception.

**Work the caller waits on that is not part of the operation.** A final interceptor is awaited
inside the dispatch, so slow work here slows every call. Queue it instead.

A post-interceptor runs after the handler returned successfully, and may replace the result. It
does not run when the handler threw; that is the exception stage.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandPostInterceptor                          // every command, untyped result
ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>                // one command, untyped result
ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>       // one command, typed result
```

Queries have the same three. Events have the first two — a broadcast produces no result.

## Observing

```csharp
public sealed class CountSuccesses(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandPostInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        metrics.Increment(command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Return the result you were given. Whatever you return is what the next stage — and eventually
the caller — sees.

## Reshaping

The typed form is the one worth reaching for when you actually touch the result:

```csharp
public sealed class CacheProduct(IMemoryCache cache)
    : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, ProductDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        cache.Set(query.Id, result, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Replacing the result is the same shape — return something else:

```csharp
public sealed class Redact : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto>
{
    public ValueTask<CustomerDto> HandleAsync(
        GetCustomer query, CustomerDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(result with { Email = "***" });
}
```

## Void pipelines

A command with no result still has a result slot: it carries `Unit`. That is what lets a
post-interceptor written against a void command receive something well-defined:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCompletion : ICommandPostInterceptor<ArchiveOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ArchiveOrder command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Archived {Id}", command.Id);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

`Unit` is a value, not `null`. Return what you were handed.

## Ordering

`[Weight]` descending, direct before covariant — the same rule as every stage. When results are
being transformed, order matters more than usual, because each interceptor sees what the last
one returned:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Redact  : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto> { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Envelope: IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto> { … }
```

## Typical uses

**Caching**, populating on the way out while a pre-interceptor checks on the way in.

**Enveloping** a result into a transport shape, when you would otherwise write it in every
handler.

**Metrics and audit** for successful operations specifically — the failure side belongs to the
exception or final stage.

**Appending to an outbox** — though be careful about ordering relative to a transaction commit;
see the [Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## What does not belong here

**Cleanup.** A post-interceptor does not run when the handler threw. Anything that must happen
either way goes in a final interceptor.

**Failure translation.** That is the exception stage, which can answer the dispatch with a
result.

**Work that should be part of the result.** If the handler's result is incomplete without this
interceptor, the handler is incomplete. An interceptor that every caller depends on is not
cross-cutting — it is the operation.

A pre-interceptor runs before the handler and returns the message the next stage will see. It
is the stage for anything that decides whether the work should happen, or fixes up its input
first.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandPreInterceptor                    // every command
ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>          // one command, or one hierarchy
```

Queries and events have the same pair — `IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — and
they behave identically.

## Observing

The untyped form takes the base marker and returns it. Return the input unchanged when you are
only watching:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Rewriting

The typed form returns the typed message, so a rewrite needs no cast:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

What the next stage receives is what you returned. Chain two of them and the second sees the
first's output.

Returning a **derived** message is allowed, and is often the point — `TCommand` is the contract,
and anything assignable to it satisfies it:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command, _clock.UtcNow));
}
```

The handler still receives it as a `PlaceOrder`. A handler that wants the enriched shape should
declare the enriched type as its message instead.

{% aside type="note" %}
Earlier previews had `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` for exactly this. It was
removed once the one-parameter form started returning the typed message, because the second
argument then bought nothing. Migration is a deletion: drop the second type argument.
{% /aside %}

## Rejecting

To stop the dispatch, abort:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant(ITenantAccessor tenant) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("no tenant on this request");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

`Abort` throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, which the pipeline treats as a stop rather than a
failure: **the exception stage does not run, and neither does the final stage.** The signal
travels to the caller unchanged.

Throwing your own exception instead is a different statement — that says the dispatch *failed*,
and it runs the failure path. Use whichever you actually mean.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Because an abort skips the final stage, anything a pre-interceptor opened — a transaction, a
lease — will not be closed there. Put the check ahead of whatever opens the resource with a
higher `[Weight]`, or open it somewhere a `try`/`finally` covers.
{% /aside %}

## Passing information forward

The message is not the only channel. When a later stage needs something that does not belong in
the message, use the context:

```csharp
public sealed class Correlate : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("correlationId", Guid.NewGuid());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Ordering

Within the stage: `[Weight]` descending, direct registrations before covariant ones. Be explicit
whenever two interceptors genuinely depend on the order:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Validate  : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
```

## Typical uses

**Validation.** Reject early, before the handler touches anything:

```csharp
public sealed class ValidateOrders : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (command.Lines.Count == 0)
            context.Abort("an order needs at least one line");

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

**Authorization**, at a high weight so it runs first.

**Normalisation** — trimming, casing, defaulting — so every handler downstream sees clean input.

**Correlation and timing**, paired with a final interceptor that reads what this one set.

## Scope it narrowly

An interceptor written against `ICommand` runs for every command in the application, including
nested dispatches. Narrow it to a base type you own when the work is not genuinely universal —
you get the typed message as well, which is worth having on its own.

This walks the whole surface once. Everything here compiles against the preview packages
described in [Installation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/installation).

## 1. Register Ergosfare

One call at the composition root. `AddGenerated()` is written by the source generator and
names every construct it found:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

Register only the modules you use. A project with no events needs no event module.

## 2. A command

A command is an instruction. It has exactly one handler, and it either produces a result or it
does not:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = new Product(command.Name, command.Price);
        await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
        return product.Id;
    }
}
```

The handler is an ordinary class. Its constructor parameters are resolved from the dispatching
scope, so a scoped `IProductRepository` behaves exactly as it does in a controller.

Dispatch it:

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (CreateProduct command, ICommandMediator mediator) =>
{
    var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command);
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});
```

For a command with no result, implement `ICommand` and `ICommandHandler<TCommand>`, and the
handler returns a plain `ValueTask`.

## 3. A query

A query asks a question and changes nothing:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<ProductDto>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
```

For a sequence, declare `IStreamQuery<T>` and implement `IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, T>`. Its
handler returns `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and the caller reads it with `await foreach`.

## 4. An event

An event is something that already happened. Every matching subscriber runs, and none of them
answers the publisher:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name);

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync($"Product {@event.Name} created", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Notice that `ProductCreated` implements nothing. Writing the subscriber is what makes it an
event, so the project that declares your domain types needs no Ergosfare reference.

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, name));
```

{% aside type="note" %}
A publish nobody subscribes to is a build error, not a silent no-op. If you write the event and
forget the subscriber, the compiler says so with `ERGO005` rather than letting the call return
having delivered nothing.
{% /aside %}

## 5. An interceptor

Interceptors are how cross-cutting work attaches without the handler knowing. This one runs
before every command:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

It needs no registration of its own — `AddGenerated()` already found it. Narrow it to one
message by implementing `ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>`, which also lets you return the
typed message:

```csharp
public sealed class TrimName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

There are four stages — pre, post, exception and final — covered in
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).

## 6. Build it

Build, and read what the compiler tells you. Ergosfare reports what it can prove:

```
error ERGO005: This dispatch of 'ArchiveProduct' can never reach a handler…
warning ERGO007: Handler 'ReindexProduct' handles 'ReindexProduct', but no dispatch site in
the compiled closure can deliver that message — the handler never executes.
```

Neither is a lint suggestion. The first says a call will always fail; the second says code you
wrote will never run. See
[Working with diagnostics](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/diagnostics) for how to read
them, and when suppressing one is the right answer.

## Where to go next

- [Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/configuring) — result
  adapters, memoized handlers, discovery keys.
- [Compile-time discovery](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery) — what
  the generator can prove, and what it declines to guess.
- [Best practices](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/messages-and-handlers) — shaping
  messages so the compiler can help you.

A queue consumer reads bytes and has to turn them into a dispatch. The mechanics are
straightforward; the parts worth getting right are the DI scope, the acknowledgement boundary,
and keeping enough static type information that the compiler can still help you.

## The shape

```csharp
public sealed class OrderConsumer(
    IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory,
    ILogger<OrderConsumer> logger) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await foreach (var delivery in _channel.ReadAllAsync(stoppingToken))
        {
            using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
            var mediator = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICommandMediator>();

            try
            {
                var command = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(delivery.Body.Span)!;
                await mediator.SendAsync(command, stoppingToken);
                await _channel.BasicAckAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                logger.LogError(ex, "delivery {Tag} failed", delivery.DeliveryTag);
                await _channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false, stoppingToken);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## One scope per message

This is the part to get right. A `BackgroundService` is a singleton, so it cannot inject a
mediator directly — the handler's scoped dependencies would come from the root scope and live
forever.

```csharp
using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
var mediator = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICommandMediator>();
```

One scope per delivery gives each message its own `DbContext`, its own unit of work, and
disposal when it finishes — the same lifetime an HTTP request would get.

## Keep the static type as specific as you can

A consumer that knows what it deserialized should dispatch through the specific mediator:

```csharp
var command = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(delivery.Body.Span)!;
await mediator.SendAsync(command, stoppingToken);
```

That keeps the dispatch provable: the compiler sees `PlaceOrder`, so `ERGO005` still applies if
nothing handles it.

A consumer that dispatches whatever arrives loses that:

```csharp
IMessage message = Deserialize(delivery);
await messageMediator.DispatchAsync(message, stoppingToken);   // opaque
```

This works, and sometimes it is genuinely what you want — a replay tool, a dead-letter drain.
But the static type proves nothing about which message arrives, so reachability can only reason
in aggregate. Turn on `ERGO009` if you want those sites flagged:

```ini
dotnet_diagnostic.ERGO009.severity = warning
```

## Routing by message type

The usual shape is a routing key or a header naming the type. Keep the mapping explicit rather
than resolving a `Type` by name — a computed type cannot be dispatched provably, and if it
reaches a `Register` call it is `ERGO018`:

```csharp
private static readonly Dictionary<string, Func<ReadOnlyMemory<byte>, ICommand>> Routes = new()
{
    ["order.place"]  = body => JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(body.Span)!,
    ["order.cancel"] = body => JsonSerializer.Deserialize<CancelOrder>(body.Span)!,
};
```

```csharp
if (!Routes.TryGetValue(delivery.RoutingKey, out var deserialize))
{
    await _channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false, stoppingToken);
    continue;
}

await mediator.SendAsync(deserialize(delivery.Body), stoppingToken);
```

Each entry names its type in source, so every one of those messages is still visible to the
compiler.

## Acknowledge after the dispatch

Ack when the handler returned, nack when it threw. Acknowledging first turns any failure into
silent data loss.

Whether to requeue is a decision the recipe cannot make for you: requeueing a message that fails
deterministically is an infinite loop. A dead-letter exchange with `requeue: false` is the
common answer.

## Cross-cutting work belongs in interceptors

Resist putting retry, logging and validation in the consumer loop — they apply to every message
and they are what interceptors are for:

```csharp
[Group("queue")]
public sealed class LogQueueDispatches : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, succeeded: exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command, GroupSet.Of("queue"), stoppingToken);
```

The group keeps the queue's pipeline separate from the one an HTTP request gets, without
duplicating a handler.

## Memoized handlers, maybe

A consumer whose handlers are genuinely stateless and whose dependencies are singletons can skip
per-dispatch resolution:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .ForceMemoizedHandlers()
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Only if that is really true. It keeps resolved participants, so a handler depending on anything
scoped captures an instance that outlives the scope that produced it. Most consumers touch a
database, which makes this the wrong choice.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing what happened

If the handler announces its work with an event, publish **after** the state is committed —
otherwise a subscriber can act on something that later rolls back. See the
[Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## Shutting down

Pass `stoppingToken` into the dispatch so a shutdown cancels in-flight work rather than being
waited out. If a message must not be interrupted halfway, do not pass it — but then nack on
shutdown so the broker redelivers.

A handler saves changes; something has to commit them. Doing that in every handler is the work
an interceptor exists to remove — and doing it *correctly* means being careful about when the
events go out.

## The shape

One transaction per command, opened before the handler and committed after it. A query gets
none, because it changes nothing.

```csharp
public interface IUnitOfWork
{
    ValueTask<IAsyncDisposable> BeginAsync(CancellationToken ct);
    ValueTask CommitAsync(CancellationToken ct);
}
```

## Marking the commands that want one

Not every command needs a transaction. A marker interface makes the choice explicit and lets
the interceptor narrow to it:

```csharp
public interface ITransactionalCommand : ICommand;

public sealed record PlaceOrder(Guid CustomerId, IReadOnlyList<Line> Lines)
    : ITransactionalCommand;
```

## The interceptors

A pre-interceptor opens, a final interceptor closes. Two stages, because the final stage runs
whether the handler succeeded or threw:

```csharp
public sealed class BeginTransaction(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork)
    : ICommandPreInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand>
{
    public async ValueTask<ITransactionalCommand> HandleAsync(
        ITransactionalCommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var transaction = await unitOfWork.BeginAsync(context.CancellationToken);
        context.Set("uow.transaction", transaction);
        return command;
    }
}
```

```csharp
public sealed class CompleteTransaction(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork, IEventMediator events)
    : ICommandFinalInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(
        ITransactionalCommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!context.TryGet<IAsyncDisposable>("uow.transaction", out var transaction))
            return;

        await using (transaction)
        {
            if (exception is not null)
                return;                                   // dispose rolls back

            await unitOfWork.CommitAsync(context.CancellationToken);
        }

        await PublishPendingAsync(context);
    }
}
```

The final stage is the right place for the close because it runs on both paths. Disposing
without committing is the rollback, so there is no separate failure branch to keep in sync.

{% aside type="caution" %}
A final interceptor does **not** run after `context.Abort()`. An abort is a deliberate stop, not
a failure, and it skips the exception and final stages — so a transaction opened in the pre
stage would leak.

Either abort *before* opening one (put authorization ahead of `BeginTransaction` with a higher
`[Weight]`), or open the transaction in the handler where a `try`/`finally` covers every exit.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing events after the commit

This is the part that is easy to get wrong. Publishing inside the transaction lets a subscriber
act on state that later rolls back:

```csharp
// the email goes out for an order that never existed
await repository.SaveAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
await events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(order.Id), context);
await unitOfWork.CommitAsync(context.CancellationToken);
```

Collect them instead, and let the final interceptor publish once the commit has succeeded:

```csharp
public static class PendingEvents
{
    private const string Key = "uow.events";

    public static void Enqueue(this ErgosfareContext context, object @event)
    {
        if (!context.TryGet<List<object>>(Key, out var pending))
            context.Set(Key, pending = []);

        pending.Add(@event);
    }

    public static IReadOnlyList<object> Drain(this ErgosfareContext context)
        => context.TryGet<List<object>>(Key, out var pending) ? pending : [];
}
```

The handler announces without dispatching:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);

        context.Enqueue(new OrderPlaced(order.Id));
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

And the final interceptor drains the queue after committing:

```csharp
private async ValueTask PublishPendingAsync(ErgosfareContext context)
{
    foreach (var @event in context.Drain())
        await events.PublishAsync(@event, context);
}
```

Passing `context` keeps the publish inside the same logical operation — same items, same
cancellation. See [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Registering it

Nothing extra. Both interceptors are discovered like any other participant:

```csharp
services.AddScoped<IUnitOfWork, EfUnitOfWork>();

services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

Order the pre stage explicitly if anything else must run before the transaction opens:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize         : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class BeginTransaction  : ICommandPreInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand> { … }
```

## When to reach for an outbox instead

Publishing after the commit closes the "subscriber saw a rolled-back order" hole. It does not
close the one where the process dies between the commit and the publish — the order exists and
nobody was told.

If that matters, write the events into the same transaction as an outbox table and let a
separate reader publish them. The interceptor above becomes the thing that *writes* the outbox
rows rather than the thing that publishes, and the shape stays the same.

## What this does not do

**It does not nest.** An inner dispatch shares the scope, so it shares the transaction. Nothing
here opens a second one, and nothing commits early.

**It does not decide isolation.** That belongs to your `IUnitOfWork`.

**It does not apply to queries.** They carry no marker, so the interceptor never matches them.

Ergosfare is an in-process mediator; it does not talk to brokers. That is a feature: the
broker client stays a thin adapter at the edge, and everything behind `PublishAsync` —
covariant handler matching, interceptors, groups, execution context — applies to a message
that arrived over the wire exactly as it applies to one raised in process.

This recipe builds that adapter for RabbitMQ: a `BackgroundService` that consumes a queue
and publishes each delivery into the event pipeline. It uses the async-first
`RabbitMQ.Client` 7.x API.

```bash
dotnet add package RabbitMQ.Client
```

## The envelope

A queue carries bytes, so something on the wire has to say which event a delivery is.
A minimal envelope carries the event's name and its serialized payload:

```csharp
public sealed record EventEnvelope(string EventType, JsonElement Payload);
```

`JsonElement` keeps the payload as parsed JSON instead of a doubly-encoded string — one
parse for the envelope, one targeted deserialization for the payload, no intermediate
`string` allocation.

## The type allow-list

Do not resolve `EventType` with `Type.GetType(...)`. A queue is an input boundary:
resolving arbitrary type names from message content lets anyone who can write to the queue
pick which types your process instantiates, and it breaks under trimming, where unreferenced
types no longer exist. An explicit allow-list solves both:

```csharp
public sealed class WireEventRegistry
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, Type> _types;

    public WireEventRegistry(params Type[] eventTypes)
        => _types = eventTypes.ToDictionary(static t => t.Name, static t => t);

    public Type? Resolve(string eventType) => _types.GetValueOrDefault(eventType);
}
```

Every type in the list is referenced by a `typeof` at registration, so the trimmer keeps it
and the boundary only ever materializes events you chose.

## The consumer

The skeleton: one connection and channel for the service's lifetime, a durable queue, and
manual acks — the broker must not consider a delivery done before the pipeline ran.

```csharp
public sealed class RabbitMqEventConsumer(
    IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory,
    WireEventRegistry wireEvents,
    IConnectionFactory connectionFactory,
    string queueName) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await using var connection = await connectionFactory.CreateConnectionAsync(stoppingToken);
        await using var channel = await connection.CreateChannelAsync(cancellationToken: stoppingToken);

        await channel.QueueDeclareAsync(queueName, durable: true, exclusive: false,
            autoDelete: false, cancellationToken: stoppingToken);

        var consumer = new AsyncEventingBasicConsumer(channel);
        consumer.ReceivedAsync += (_, delivery) => HandleDeliveryAsync(channel, delivery, stoppingToken);

        await channel.BasicConsumeAsync(queueName, autoAck: false, consumer, stoppingToken);

        await Task.Delay(Timeout.Infinite, stoppingToken);
    }
}
```

## Dispatching a delivery

The interesting part. Piece by piece:

```csharp
private async Task HandleDeliveryAsync(
    IChannel channel, BasicDeliverEventArgs delivery, CancellationToken ct)
{
    try
    {
        var envelope = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<EventEnvelope>(delivery.Body.Span);

        if (envelope is null
            || wireEvents.Resolve(envelope.EventType) is not { } eventType
            || envelope.Payload.Deserialize(eventType) is not IEvent @event)
        {
            // Unknown or malformed: requeueing can only fail again. Nack without
            // requeue and let a dead-letter exchange keep the evidence.
            await channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, multiple: false,
                requeue: false, cancellationToken: ct);
            return;
        }

        using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
        var events = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IEventMediator>();

        var settings = new EventMediationSettings();
        settings.Items["rabbitmq:deliveryTag"] = delivery.DeliveryTag;
        settings.Items["rabbitmq:redelivered"] = delivery.Redelivered;

        await events.PublishAsync(@event, settings, ct);

        await channel.BasicAckAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, multiple: false, cancellationToken: ct);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Shutting down: leave the delivery unacked and the broker redelivers it.
    }
    catch
    {
        // A handler or interceptor threw. First failure gets one retry; a redelivered
        // failure goes to the dead-letter exchange instead of looping forever.
        await channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, multiple: false,
            requeue: !delivery.Redelivered, cancellationToken: CancellationToken.None);
    }
}
```

**A scope per delivery.** Handlers resolve from the provider of the scope that dispatched
them, so a scope per delivery gives every message the same isolation an ASP.NET request
gets — scoped `DbContext`s and unit-of-work services just work. This is the shape the
dispatch path is optimized for: resolving `IEventMediator` builds a single facade object
over a shared dispatch engine, and the scoped-dispatch numbers on the
[performance page](/ergosfare.docs/advanced/performance/) include exactly this cost.

**The runtime type decides the pipeline.** The variable is typed `IEvent`, so this takes
the interface-erased publish path: the pipeline is resolved from `@event.GetType()`, and
covariant handlers match as usual. Polymorphic dispatch at the boundary is first-class,
not a degraded mode.

**Transport metadata rides the context.** Anything put into `Settings.Items` is visible to
every handler and interceptor in the dispatch through the execution context:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderPlacedHandler : IEventHandler<OrderPlaced>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderPlaced @event, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<bool>("rabbitmq:redelivered", out var redelivered) && redelivered)
        {
            // second attempt — be idempotent about side effects
        }

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

**Ack only after the pipeline.** `PublishAsync` completes when every matched handler and
interceptor has run, so the ack really means "processed". The nack policy is deliberately
boring: malformed and unknown messages never requeue, handler failures requeue exactly
once. Declare the queue with a dead-letter exchange so nacked deliveries land somewhere
inspectable instead of vanishing.

## Wiring

```csharp
builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o.AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));

builder.Services.AddSingleton<IConnectionFactory>(new ConnectionFactory { HostName = "localhost" });
builder.Services.AddSingleton(new WireEventRegistry(typeof(OrderPlaced), typeof(OrderCancelled)));

builder.Services.AddHostedService(sp => new RabbitMqEventConsumer(
    sp.GetRequiredService<IServiceScopeFactory>(),
    sp.GetRequiredService<WireEventRegistry>(),
    sp.GetRequiredService<IConnectionFactory>(),
    queueName: "orders"));
```

The events themselves are ordinary Ergosfare events — `RegisterGenerated()` discovers their
handlers at compile time like any other; nothing about arriving over a queue changes their
registration.

## Notes

* **No handlers is a silent success by default.** An event nobody handles completes
  normally and gets acked. If an unhandled wire event should be an error in your system,
  publish with `new EventMediationSettings { ThrowIfNoHandlerFound = true }` and the nack
  path takes over.
* **Ordering and concurrency.** One consumer processes deliveries sequentially. For
  parallelism, raise the channel's prefetch and run multiple consumers — per-delivery
  scopes already isolate them from each other.
* **Native AOT.** The dispatch side is AOT-clean (see
  [Native AOT and trimming](/ergosfare.docs/advanced/aot-and-trimming/)), but
  `JsonSerializer.Deserialize(Type)` here is reflection-based serialization — on AOT,
  switch the envelope handling to a source-generated `JsonSerializerContext`.

Bu sayfa minimal ama eksiksiz bir kurulumu adım adım anlatıyor: bir command, bir query, bir
event ve hepsini tek satır elle kayıt olmadan kaydeden başlangıç kodu.

## Adım 1 — Kurulum

```bash
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator
```

## Adım 2 — Bir command ve handler'ı tanımlayın

Command, bir şey *yaptıran* mesajdır. Sonuç üretebilir de üretmeyebilir de; ürettiğinde
`ICommand<TResult>` kullanın.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public CreateProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Dikkat edilmesi gereken iki nokta:

- Handler **`ValueTask<TResult>`** döndürür. Senkron tamamlanan bir handler hiçbir allocation
  yapmaz.
- İptal token'ı ayrı bir parametre olarak değil, **context üzerinden** gelir. Onu iletmeyi
  unutmanız mümkün değildir.

## Adım 3 — Bir query ve handler'ı tanımlayın

Query bir şey *sorar* ve her zaman bir sonuç üretir.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<Product?>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Adım 4 — Bir event ve handler'larını tanımlayın

Event yayınlanır: eşleşen her handler çalışır, hiç handler olmayabilir de.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name) : IEvent;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}

public sealed class UpdateSearchIndex : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _index.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Adım 5 — Hepsini kaydedin

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));
```

Kaydın tamamı bu. `RegisterGenerated()` hiçbir NuGet paketinde yoktur — kaynak üreteci onu
*sizin* derlemenize, bulduğu her handler'ı içerecek şekilde üretir. Projenin herhangi bir
yerine bir handler ekleyin, sonraki build onu alır.

{% aside type="tip" %}
`using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;` zorunludur — üreteç bu namespace'e yazar. Çözülmeyen bir
`RegisterGenerated()` neredeyse her zaman eksik bir using demektir.
{% /aside %}

## Adım 6 — Dispatch edin

İhtiyacınız olan modülün mediator arayüzünü inject edin. Ayrı olmaları bilinçlidir: yalnızca
okuma yapan bir bileşen command gönderme yeteneğini almaz.

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (
    CreateProduct command,
    ICommandMediator commands,
    IEventMediator events) =>
{
    var id = await commands.SendAsync(command);
    await events.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, command.Name));
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});

app.MapGet("/products/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, IQueryMediator queries) =>
{
    var product = await queries.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
    return product is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(product);
});
```

| Modül | Arayüz | Çağrı |
|---|---|---|
| Command | `ICommandMediator` | `SendAsync(command)` |
| Query | `IQueryMediator` | `QueryAsync(query)`, `StreamAsync(streamQuery)` |
| Event | `IEventMediator` | `PublishAsync(@event)` |

## Adım 7 — Bir cross-cutting concern ekleyin

Interceptor'lar handler'lara dokunmadan pipeline'a takılır. Bir base tip veya arayüz için
kaydedilen pre-interceptor, ona atanabilir her mesaja uygulanır:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Attributes;

[Weight(100)]                                  // yüksek ağırlık önce çalışır
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatch ediliyor: {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);   // mesajı olduğu gibi geçir
    }
}
```

Kayıt gerekmez — üreteç onu tıpkı bir handler gibi bulur.

{% aside type="note" %}
Bir pre-interceptor `void` yerine mesajı döndürür, çünkü onu *yeniden yazma* hakkı vardır:
döndürdüğünüz şey bir sonraki aşamanın gördüğü şeydir. Yukarıdaki gibi mesajı değiştirmeden
döndürmek, geçiş (pass-through) durumudur. Bkz.
[Interceptorlar](/ergosfare.docs/tr/core-concepts/interceptors).
{% /aside %}

## Sırada ne var

- [Derleme zamanı keşif](/ergosfare.docs/tr/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery) — üreteç ne görüyor, nasıl kontrol edilir.
- [Interceptorlar](/ergosfare.docs/tr/core-concepts/interceptors) — dört aşama ve nasıl birleştikleri.
- [Yürütme bağlamı](/ergosfare.docs/tr/core-concepts/execution-context) — havuzlama, yaşam süresi ve getirdiği kurallar.
- [İç içe dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/tr/core-concepts/nested-dispatch) — handler içinden mediator çağırmak.

Her handler ve interceptor bir `ErgosfareContext` alır. İptal token'ını, dispatch başına bir
durum torbasını ve bir pipeline'ı durdurmanın yolunu taşır.

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    …
}
```

## İptal

`context.CancellationToken`, dispatch'in başlatıldığı token'dır. `CancellationToken.None`'a
uzanmak yerine onu aşağı geçirin:

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
```

## Dispatch başına durum

`Items`, tek bir dispatch'teki her katılımcının paylaştığı bir sözlüktür. Bir
pre-interceptor'ın mesajı değiştirmeden sonraki bir aşamaya bir şey söyleme yoludur:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Erişimciler:

| Üye | Ne yapar |
|---|---|
| `Set(key, value)` | Bir değer saklar. |
| `Has(key)` | Anahtarın var olup olmadığı. |
| `Get<T>(key)` | Okur; yoksa ya da tip yanlışsa fırlatır. |
| `TryGet<T>(key, out value)` | Fırlatmadan okur. |
| `Items` | Yukarıdakilerin kapsamadığı her şey için alttaki sözlük. |

{% aside type="caution" %}
`Items` dispatch başınadır, istek başına değil. İç içe bir dispatch, siz açıkça geçirmedikçe
kendi context'ini alır; dolayısıyla bir HTTP istek scope'una ait olması gereken bir şey için
yanlış yerdir. Onu scoped bir servise koyup enjekte edin.
{% /aside %}

## Pipeline'ı durdurmak

`Abort()` dispatch'i hemen bitirir:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (_tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("bu istekte tenant yok");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

`ExecutionAbortedException` fırlatır ve pipeline bunu bir başarısızlık değil bir durdurma olarak
ele alır: **exception aşaması çalışmaz, final aşaması da çalışmaz.** Sinyal çağırana olduğu gibi
gider.

Bu ayrım, ayrı bir sinyale sahip olmanın bütün amacıdır. Başarısızlık ters giden ve bir final
interceptor'ın yine de görmesi gereken bir şeydir; durdurma ise bu dispatch'in devam etmemesi
gerektiğine dair bir karardır.

Aşırı yüklemeler bir sebep ve bir değer taşır:

```csharp
context.Abort("yetkili değil");
context.Abort("kota aşıldı", kalanKota);
```

## Bir context'i aktarmak

Her dispatch yüzeyi isteğe bağlı bir context alır. Kendinizinkini geçirmek, iç içe dispatch'in
`Items`'ınızı ve iptalinizi paylaşmasını sağlar:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(command.Id), context);
}
```

Geçirmemek iç dispatch'e taze bir context verir. Hangisini istediğiniz, iç işin aynı mantıksal
işlemin parçası olup olmadığına bağlıdır — bkz.
[İç içe dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/tr/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Scope'lar

`CreateScope()` tek bir dispatch içinde iç içe bir yaşam süresi verir; pipeline'ın bir bölümünden
uzun yaşamaması gereken durum için:

```csharp
using var scope = context.CreateScope();
```

## Neden havuzlanıyor

Context'ler havuzlanır ve dispatch tamamlandığında geri verilir. Bir dispatch'in sıcak yolda
çağrı başına bir tane ayırmasını engelleyen şey budur, ve bilinmesi gereken tek bir sonucu
vardır:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Katılımcınız döndükten sonra bir context'i saklamayın.** Onu bir alanda tutmak, arka plan
görevinde kapatmak ya da dispatch'ten uzun yaşayan bir şeye vermek, havuza çoktan geri verilmiş
ve artık ilgisiz bir dispatch'e ait olabilecek bir nesneyi tutuyorsunuz demektir. Bunun yerine
ihtiyacınız olan değerleri kopyalayın.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// yanlış — bu çalıştığında context çoktan gitmiş olur
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(context.Get<string>("user")));

// doğru — önce okuyun
var user = context.Get<string>("user");
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(user));
```

Havuzlama, dispatch yolunda hiçbir yerde `AsyncLocal` olmamasının da sebebidir: context bir
parametredir, dolayısıyla tam olarak geçirdiğiniz kadar akar, daha ileri değil.

Interceptor'lar, kesişen işlerin handler'ın haberi olmadan bir dispatch'e bağlandığı yerdir.
Handler'lar gibi keşfedilir ve kaydedilirler, ve aynı derlenmiş pipeline'ın parçası olarak
çalışırlar.

## Dört aşama

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** handler'dan önce çalışır ve mesajı yeniden yazabilir.
- **post** başarılı bir handler'dan sonra çalışır ve sonucu yeniden yazabilir.
- **exception** handler ya da bir interceptor fırlattığında çalışır ve bunun yerine bir sonuç
  verebilir.
- **final** her zaman en son çalışır; başarıda da başarısızlıkta da.

Bir aşama içinde sıra `[Weight]` azalan, doğrudan kayıtlar kovaryant olanlardan öncedir. Bkz.
[Gruplar ve ağırlıklar](/ergosfare.docs/tr/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptorlar

Bir pre-interceptor, bir sonraki aşamanın göreceği mesajı döndürür. Girdiyi değiştirmeden
döndürmek, geçiş durumudur:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("{Command} dispatch ediliyor", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Genişlik ile tip güvenliğini takas eden iki arite var:

| Arayüz | `HandleAsync` imzası | Ne zaman |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, ErgosfareContext)` | her command'a uyguluyorsa |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, ErgosfareContext)` | tek bir command ya da hiyerarşiye uyguluyorsa |

Tipli form tipli mesajı döndürür, dolayısıyla yeniden yazmak için cast gerekmez:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

*Türetilmiş* bir mesaj döndürmek de serbesttir ve çoğu zaman amaç budur — `TCommand` bir
sözleşmedir ve ona atanabilen her şey onu karşılar.

{% aside type="note" %}
Önceki önizlemelerde iki parametreli bir form vardı: `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>`.
Kaldırıldı: tek parametreli form tipli mesajı döndürmeye başladığından beri ikinci argüman hiçbir
şey satın almıyordu. Geçiş bir silme işlemidir — ikinci tip argümanını atın, interceptor
döndürdüğünü döndürmeye devam eder.
{% /aside %}

## Post-interceptorlar

Bir post-interceptor başarılı bir sonucu görür ve onu değiştirebilir:

```csharp
public sealed class StampCreatedAt : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Üç arite: `ICommandPostInterceptor`, `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` ve
`ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>`.

Sonuç üretmeyen bir pipeline'da sonuç slot'u `Unit` taşır; sonucu olmayan bir command'a karşı
yazılmış bir post-interceptor'ın yine de iyi tanımlı bir şey alması bunu sağlar.

## Exception interceptorlar

Bir exception interceptor bir şey fırlattığında çalışır ve exception'ın kaçmasına izin vermek
yerine dispatch'i bir sonuçla cevaplayabilir:

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct command, ProductDto? result, Exception exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (exception is NotFoundException)
            return ValueTask.FromResult(ProductDto.Empty);

        throw exception;
    }
}
```

Asimetriye dikkat, bilinçli: **sonuç nullable girer, non-nullable çıkar.** Handler bir şey
üretmeden fırlatmış olabilir, yani gerçekten henüz sonuç olmayabilir — ama bir dispatch sonuç
tipini çağrı yerinde kilitlemiştir ve aşağıdaki hiçbir aşama çağırana istemediği bir `null`
veremez.

Sonuç üretemeyen bir interceptor hatayı sahiplenmemelidir. Yeniden fırlatın ya da eşleşmeyin;
eşleşmeyen bir aşama exception'ın yükselmesine izin verir. Yokluğu modellemek isteyen kod bunu
değerin içinde modeller — `Result<T>` bunun içindir.

Herhangi bir exception interceptor çalıştığında exception ele alınmış sayılır ve pipeline final
aşamasına devam eder. Hiçbiri eşleşmediyse yükselir.

### Exception tipine göre filtreleme

`For` varyantını implemente etmek bir interceptor'ı tek bir exception tipine daraltır; bu bir
`is` kontrolünden daha iyi okunur ve derlenmiş planın testi gömmesini sağlar:

```csharp
public sealed class HandleTimeouts
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptorFor<PlaceOrder, Guid, TimeoutException>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, TimeoutException exception, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(Guid.Empty);
}
```

## Final interceptorlar

Bir final interceptor her zaman çalışır — başarıdan, ele alınmış bir hatadan ve ele alınmamış bir
hatadan sonra. Hiçbir şey döndürmez, çünkü o noktada dispatch'in cevabı çoktan bellidir:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Burada hem sonuç hem exception nullable'dır, aynı sebeple: bir `finally`'den çalışır, dolayısıyla
ikisi de yok olabilir.

Çalışmadığı tek durum bilinçli bir durdurmadır — aşağıya bakın.

## Bir pipeline'ı durdurmak

Herhangi bir katılımcı `ExecutionAbortedException` fırlatarak dispatch'i erkenden bitirebilir:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.IsAuthenticated)
            throw new ExecutionAbortedException("kimlik doğrulanmadı");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Bu bir başarısızlık değildir ve öyle muamele görmez: exception aşaması çalışmaz, final aşaması
çalışmaz ve sinyal çağırana gider. "Bu dispatch devam etmemeli" der, "bu dispatch ters gitti"
demez.

## Hangi modülün sözleşmeleri

Her modülün kendi interceptor sözleşmeleri vardır — `ICommandPreInterceptor`,
`IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — ve o modülün marker'ını taşırlar; keşfin onları
bulma şekli budur.

Gerçekten evrensel bir şey için `Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Handlers` içindeki çekirdek
sözleşmeleri `IMessage`'a karşı implemente edin. Bu her modülün mesajlarına ulaşır, karşılığında
daha zayıf bir statik tip alırsınız.

## Maliyeti

İstemediğiniz hiçbir şey, dispatch başına. Aşamalar derleme zamanında kurulur ve şekli tamamen
oturmuş bir pipeline için üretilen plan, interceptor'ları yerinde olan düz bir çağrı dizisidir —
descriptor listesi yok, sıralama işi yok, ve doğrudan kurulabildiği kanıtlanan katılımcılar için
container çözümü de yok.

Hiç interceptor'ı olmayan bir pipeline hiçbirinin bedelini ödemez: aynı düz çizgi, çağrılar
olmadan.

Bir pre-interceptor handler'dan önce çalışır ve bir sonraki aşamanın göreceği mesajı döndürür.
İşin olup olmayacağına karar veren ya da girdisini önce düzelten her şey için doğru aşamadır.

## Sözleşmeler

```csharp
ICommandPreInterceptor                    // her command
ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>          // tek command ya da tek hiyerarşi
```

Query ve event'lerin aynı ikilisi vardır — `IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — ve
aynı şekilde davranırlar.

## Gözlemlemek

Tipsiz form base marker'ı alır ve onu döndürür. Yalnız izliyorsanız girdiyi değiştirmeden
döndürün:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("{Command} dispatch ediliyor", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Yeniden yazmak

Tipli form tipli mesajı döndürür, dolayısıyla yeniden yazma cast gerektirmez:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

Bir sonraki aşamanın aldığı şey, döndürdüğünüz şeydir. İkisini zincirleyin, ikincisi birincinin
çıktısını görür.

**Türetilmiş** bir mesaj döndürmek serbesttir ve çoğu zaman amaç budur — `TCommand` bir
sözleşmedir ve ona atanabilen her şey onu karşılar:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command, _clock.UtcNow));
}
```

Handler onu yine bir `PlaceOrder` olarak alır. Zenginleştirilmiş şekli isteyen bir handler,
zenginleştirilmiş tipi kendi mesajı olarak bildirmelidir.

{% aside type="note" %}
Önceki önizlemelerde tam olarak bunun için `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` vardı.
Tek parametreli form tipli mesajı döndürmeye başlayınca ikinci argüman hiçbir şey satın almadığı
için kaldırıldı. Geçiş bir silme işlemidir: ikinci tip argümanını atın.
{% /aside %}

## Reddetmek

Dispatch'i durdurmak için abort edin:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant(ITenantAccessor tenant) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("bu istekte tenant yok");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

`Abort`, pipeline'ın bir başarısızlık değil durdurma olarak ele aldığı
`ExecutionAbortedException` fırlatır: **exception aşaması çalışmaz, final aşaması da çalışmaz.**
Sinyal çağırana olduğu gibi gider.

Kendi exception'ınızı fırlatmak farklı bir ifadedir — o, dispatch'in *başarısız olduğunu* söyler
ve hata yolunu çalıştırır. Hangisini kastediyorsanız onu kullanın.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Abort final aşamasını atladığı için, bir pre-interceptor'ın açtığı bir şey — transaction, kilit —
orada kapatılmayacaktır. Kontrolü, kaynağı açan şeyin önüne daha yüksek bir `[Weight]` ile
koyun, ya da kaynağı bir `try`/`finally`'nin kapsadığı bir yerde açın.
{% /aside %}

## Bilgiyi ileri taşımak

Mesaj tek kanal değildir. Sonraki bir aşamanın mesaja ait olmayan bir şeye ihtiyacı olduğunda
context'i kullanın:

```csharp
context.Set("correlationId", Guid.NewGuid());
```

## Sıralama

Aşama içinde: `[Weight]` azalan, doğrudan kayıtlar kovaryant olanlardan önce. İki interceptor
gerçekten sıraya bağlıysa açıkça söyleyin:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Validate  : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
```

## Tipik kullanımlar

**Doğrulama.** Handler bir şeye dokunmadan erken reddedin.

**Yetkilendirme**, önce çalışsın diye yüksek bir ağırlıkla.

**Normalleştirme** — kırpma, harf düzeltme, varsayılan atama — böylece aşağıdaki her handler temiz
girdi görür.

**Korelasyon ve zamanlama**, bunun koyduğunu okuyan bir final interceptor ile eşleştirilmiş.

## Dar tutun

`ICommand`'a karşı yazılmış bir interceptor uygulamadaki her command için çalışır, iç içe
dispatch'ler dahil. İş gerçekten evrensel değilse onu sahip olduğunuz bir base tipe daraltın —
üstelik tipli mesajı da alırsınız, ki bu tek başına değerlidir.

Bu sayfa yüzeyin tamamını bir kez yürüyor. Buradaki her şey
[Kurulum](/ergosfare.docs/tr/preview/getting-started/installation)'da anlatılan önizleme
paketlerine karşı derleniyor.

## 1. Ergosfare'yi kaydedin

Composition root'ta tek bir çağrı. `AddGenerated()` kaynak üreteci tarafından yazılır ve
bulduğu her yapıyı adıyla sayar:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

Yalnız kullandığınız modülleri kaydedin. Event'i olmayan bir uygulamanın event modülüne ihtiyacı
yoktur.

## 2. Bir command

Command bir talimattır. Tam olarak bir handler'ı vardır ve ya bir sonuç üretir ya da üretmez:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = new Product(command.Name, command.Price);
        await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
        return product.Id;
    }
}
```

Handler sıradan bir sınıftır. Kurucu parametreleri dispatch eden scope'tan çözülür, yani scoped
bir `IProductRepository` tam olarak bir controller'daki gibi davranır.

Dispatch edin:

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (CreateProduct command, ICommandMediator mediator) =>
{
    var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command);
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});
```

Sonuç üretmeyen bir command için `ICommand` ve `ICommandHandler<TCommand>` implemente edin;
handler düz bir `ValueTask` döndürür.

## 3. Bir query

Query bir soru sorar ve hiçbir şeyi değiştirmez:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<ProductDto>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
```

Bir dizi için `IStreamQuery<T>` bildirin ve `IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, T>` implemente edin.
Handler'ının metodu `StreamAsync`'tir ve `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` döndürür; çağıran onu
`await foreach` ile okur.

## 4. Bir event

Event, zaten olmuş bir şeydir. Eşleşen her abone çalışır ve hiçbiri yayınlayana cevap vermez:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name);

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync($"{@event.Name} ürünü oluşturuldu", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

`ProductCreated`'in hiçbir şey implemente etmediğine dikkat edin. Onu event yapan şey aboneyi
yazmaktır; yani domain tiplerinizi bildiren projenin Ergosfare referansına ihtiyacı yoktur.

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, name));
```

{% aside type="note" %}
Kimsenin abone olmadığı bir publish sessiz bir no-op değil, derleme hatasıdır. Event'i yazıp
aboneyi unutursanız derleyici bunu `ERGO005` ile söyler; çağrının hiçbir şey teslim etmeden
dönmesine izin vermez.
{% /aside %}

## 5. Bir interceptor

Interceptor'lar, kesişen işlerin handler'ın haberi olmadan bağlandığı yerdir. Bu, her
command'dan önce çalışır:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("{Command} dispatch ediliyor", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Kendine ait bir kayıt gerekmez — `AddGenerated()` onu zaten buldu. Tek bir mesaja
daraltmak için `ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>` implemente edin; bu size tipli mesajı da
verir:

```csharp
public sealed class TrimName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

Dört aşama var — pre, post, exception ve final —
[Interceptorlar](/ergosfare.docs/tr/preview/core-concepts/interceptors)'da anlatılıyor.

## 6. Derleyin

Derleyin ve derleyicinin size söylediklerini okuyun. Ergosfare kanıtlayabildiğini raporlar:

```
error ERGO005: This dispatch of 'ArchiveProduct' can never reach a handler…
warning ERGO007: Handler 'ReindexProduct' handles 'ReindexProduct', but no dispatch site in
the compiled closure can deliver that message — the handler never executes.
```

Bunların ikisi de bir stil önerisi değil. Birincisi bir çağrının her zaman başarısız olacağını,
ikincisi yazdığınız kodun hiç çalışmayacağını söylüyor.

